WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.428
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.396 --> 00:00:08.640
NICOLAS DE CRISTOFARO:
Welcome to NET204--

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.280
connecting your data center to
cloud-- approach for mission

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.205
critical network reliability.

00:00:13.205 --> 00:00:14.580
My name is Nicolas
De Cristofaro.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.800
I'm a customer engineer
here at Google,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.310
specializing in Google's
dual cloud network.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:25.045
So first let's talk
about Google's network.

00:00:25.045 --> 00:00:26.670
So when we talk about
Google's network,

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.590
we're talking about a
massive global platform,

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:29.310
as you can see.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.970
134 points of presence,
massive investments

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:36.630
in sub-sea cables connecting
the world, as you can see behind

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:40.110
me, all within a private
backbone network that we own

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.750
or that we have private
connectivity for.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.977
The map here clearly
distincts some regions

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:47.310
that we have-- of course,
also future regions that we're

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:51.020
planning to launch, as well
as the mass point of presence

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.730
that we have to connecting
your data center to the cloud.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.920
So let's talk a bit
about how do you

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.040
connect Google to the cloud.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.290
So connecting your
place to our place,

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:06.950
we actually have five ways of
connecting Google to the cloud.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.320
We divide these into
two different sections,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.630
public addressing and
private addressing.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:14.750
The latter at the top is the
public addressing method,

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.580
which is actually
connecting Google

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.520
via various means of peering.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.140
So direct peering
is one of them,

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.833
which offers the ability to
connect to Google via standard

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:26.000
peering that you would do
with an internet exchange,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.830
essentially connecting
your public prefixes--

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.500
so address blocks
that you would own--

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.060
to Google Cloud via
standard BGP peering.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.615
On the top right-hand side,
we talk about carrier peering.

00:01:38.615 --> 00:01:40.490
Carrier peering is
essentially the same means

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:44.030
of connecting to Google via
public addressing, however

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.920
through a carrier that
provides that connectivity.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.580
So this is in case,
for example, you

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.750
do not have the connectivity
to Google directly

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.150
to be able to meet us at
our peering facilities,

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.730
you can do that
through a carrier.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.430
On the bottom part, we speak
about private addressing.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.848
Now this is really interesting
for enterprise environments

00:02:00.848 --> 00:02:02.390
where you want to
extend connectivity

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:06.080
from your on-premise environment
using private RFC 1918

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.870
addressing and connecting
directly to Google Cloud.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.970
So that means being
able to reach directly

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.790
your on-prem workloads
to Google Cloud via two

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:15.250
means of connectivity.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.070
And then of course we
have the VPN option,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.880
which sits hybrid
in the middle, which

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.520
provides a mix of connectivity
across public internet links

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.890
to do the private
addressing, extending

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.925
your on-prem environment
to the cloud.

00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:28.550
So when we speak of
private addressing,

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:31.160
we actually have two
methods of doing this.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.170
One is dedicated
interconnect, which

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:36.560
is to directly connect
to Google Cloud via one

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.390
of our various
points of presence.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.262
That means your
equipment is co-located

00:02:40.262 --> 00:02:41.720
in a point of
presence, or you have

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.640
connectivity to our
point of presence

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.750
and establishing a
dedicated link to us.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:48.140
The partner
interconnect offering

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.350
is actually to provide
connectivity, again privately,

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:53.870
however through a partner,
a service provider that

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.030
is able to extend the
last mile of connectivity

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.880
from your on-premise
environment to Google Cloud.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.830
In case you do not have
connectivity in one

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.560
of our points of presence, you
can use a partner interconnect

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.370
to do that for you.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.100
And as a hybrid
model as I mentioned,

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.130
VPN as a way to connect
via public internet links.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:11.810
And often enterprises
start that way

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.660
before actually moving on to
purchase a dedicated or partner

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:15.848
interconnect offering.

00:03:15.848 --> 00:03:17.390
And I will show you
later on actually

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:21.530
how seamlessly the transition is
done between a VPN connectivity

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.100
to Google Cloud
through a partner

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.470
or dedicated interconnect link.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.550
So speak a bit about
the cloud interconnect.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.160
But first I'm pleased to
announce that we are announcing

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.040
100 gig connections at
our POPs, so now you're

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.690
able to connect not
only with 10 gigs

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:42.830
but now with 100 gig connections
at our Points of Presence.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.660
So cloud interconnect,
as I mentioned,

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:47.980
is a method to provide
access to private RFC 1918

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.260
addressing from your
on-prem workloads

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.370
directly to the cloud.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.890
And it enables essentially
a hybrid cloud connectivity

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.000
model.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.263
It does not require the use
of management of hardware VPN

00:03:58.263 --> 00:04:00.430
devices, because this is
something that you directly

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:04.420
connect your on-premise router
to us via the various POP

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.160
locations that we're located in.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.200
So from the left-hand side here
we see dedicated interconnect,

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.160
as I mentioned.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.370
We could do 10 gigs,
now we can do 100 gigs.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:15.340
We can provide an LACP
bundle, so a link group bundle

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.480
from eight times 10 gigs
or two times 100 gigs, per

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.490
provided transport circuits
directly to our cloud

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:24.430
environment from your
on-premise data center.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.950
And a partner interconnect
provides dedicated bandwidth

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:30.010
for sub-circuit rates,
starting at 50 megabits all

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.800
the way to 10 gigs, provided
through a service provider

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:33.300
network.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.730
So here's the bit about how the
interconnect deployment looks

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:41.340
like.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.970
So we can look at a dedicated
interconnect in this way.

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.035
We have a co-location
facility in the middle.

00:04:46.035 --> 00:04:47.660
The co-location
facility is essentially

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.418
where you would meet us at
one of our points of presence.

00:04:50.418 --> 00:04:52.460
So you may either have a
cage already established

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:53.300
in one of the facilities.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.092
For example, some of
the Equinix facilities

00:04:55.092 --> 00:04:57.110
is a very common
co-location facility,

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:59.120
or any other
co-location facilities.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:00.950
Or you can extend
connectivity there

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:04.880
through any telco
providers that can extend

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:06.950
that connectivity to you.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.380
And from that you extend
your on-premise connection

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:10.400
to your data center.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.970
And we provide connectivity
to our cloud network.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.420
Now here's a list of our
interconnect locations.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.660
So as you can see, we're
present all around the world

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.180
in all the continents,
except of course Antarctica.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:26.450
And then we have connectivity
with low latency links, which

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:30.860
is the dark green circles
which are providing

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.040
very low latency access
to Google Cloud resources

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.982
if you need a low latency
access between your workloads

00:05:37.982 --> 00:05:38.690
and Google Cloud.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.690
So that means you can
provide sub-five milliseconds

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:44.750
on average from these sites to
any regions that are located

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.220
in these points of presence.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.560
Looking at the partner
interconnect offering,

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.200
this is essentially
a model where

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.960
the partner is essentially
providing the connection

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:56.620
to Google.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:59.200
So partners of established a
partner relationship with us

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:00.010
already.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:01.780
They've established
connectivity with us,

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.790
and they provide that last mile
connectivity to their clients.

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:06.680
So various partners offer that.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:08.500
And if you're not
looking for something

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.540
at 10 gigs or potentially
higher throughput,

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:12.790
you would look at a partner
to offer that because they

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:14.740
can provide sub-circuit
rates from 50 megabits

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.730
all the way up to 10 gigs.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.160
So they can provide
that solution.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:19.980
And also one thing
that some partners

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.140
are able to offer as well
is that they can minimize

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:23.140
the router requirements.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:27.130
So you may not need a router
at the co-location facility

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:27.670
themselves.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.780
In case you want to do something
like a multi-cloud scenario,

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:31.238
you want to connect
cloud to cloud,

00:06:31.238 --> 00:06:34.390
some partners offer the ability
to connect two clouds together

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:36.430
via a virtual router offering.

00:06:40.037 --> 00:06:42.370
So let's look at some of the
distinction between partner

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.080
and dedicated interconnect.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:46.840
So why would you choose partner,
why would you choose dedicated?

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:49.340
I talked to a lot of customers,
depending on their use case,

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:50.050
it's very clear.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:52.720
Partner is where you're
not able to have access

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.070
to our points of presence.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:58.150
You're not physically co-located
at a point of presence.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:00.520
You may not need to have
a requirement of 10 gigs.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.103
You don't need 10 gigs, which
is the minimum that we can offer

00:07:03.103 --> 00:07:04.360
with dedicated interconnect.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.010
Therefore a partner
will provide you

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:08.320
sub-circuit rates starting
from 50 megabits all

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:09.490
the way to 10 gigs.

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:12.640
And we can provide that via
VLAN attachments on our end.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:14.860
You pay only for what you need.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:17.830
SLA is required at least to
have two VLAN attachments, which

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:20.050
will terminate at two
different availability

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:21.568
zones within a metro area.

00:07:21.568 --> 00:07:23.860
If you want to have an SLA
of three nines and then four

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.818
nines, you would just
establish that across two

00:07:25.818 --> 00:07:27.820
different metropolitan areas.

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:30.250
You can actually also establish
with partner interconnect.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.050
You don't need to
be within one org.

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:34.787
We can actually extend the
connectivity from multiple orgs

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:36.370
because the partner
interconnect VLANs

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:38.200
can land in different
organizational units

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.650
within GCP.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:43.622
For dedicated, it's
often used by customers

00:07:43.622 --> 00:07:44.830
that require high throughput.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.140
They require a
minimum of 10 gigs.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:50.050
The customer would essentially
meet us at one of our POPs,

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:52.150
or again extend connectivity
to one of our POPs,

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:55.150
either via a service provider
or meet us physically in one

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:56.890
of our point of presence.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:58.540
All the VLANs are
actually riding

00:07:58.540 --> 00:07:59.770
on the same physical link.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.050
So that means that when you
establish VLAN attachments

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:05.110
through our cloud console,
you would actually

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:06.670
establish the VLAN
attachments within

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:10.040
the same physical circuit
that's established with us.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:13.240
SLAs again require at
least two connections

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:17.320
to one metro area across
two availability zones.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:20.800
And then all VLANs need to land
in the same organizational unit

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.630
and GCP for dedicated
interconnect.

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:26.260
That means that you can
establish a project in GCP

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:27.372
within one org.

00:08:27.372 --> 00:08:29.080
And that project can
share its resources,

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:31.090
but it's still bound
by the organization.

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:32.919
That's one of the advantages
that the partner interconnect

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:34.630
can do is you can
actually span that

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.580
across multiple
organizations, if you need to.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:42.059
So let's talk about
a few different ways

00:08:42.059 --> 00:08:43.642
that you can actually
use interconnect

00:08:43.642 --> 00:08:48.190
on some of the use cases that
our customers are using today.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:50.670
So here's just an example,
again, of the private routing

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:55.380
that you can establish between
your on-premise infrastructure

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:57.270
and our cloud environment.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.490
So essentially you would
establish a BGP peer

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:02.340
via our offering, which is
called a Cloud Router, which

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:04.530
would be deployed
in the cloud region,

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:07.650
and then establish BGP peering
with your on-premise router

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:08.760
and exchanging prefixes.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.400
In this case here, we have a
headquarter with an external

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:17.330
data center that has a
network of 192.168.55.0/24,

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:20.160
and we're advertising
that to the cloud region.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.970
And the cloud region is
receiving that prefix over BGP,

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:27.270
and the cloud region
has 192.168.49.0/24,

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.020
and we're advertising
that across.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:30.660
So we can establish
that connectivity

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:33.600
through the Cloud Router,
which is our virtual BGP

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.260
speaker in the cloud and
you on-premise BGP speaker.

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:41.580
Another use case
that we've seen is,

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.627
because of the concept
of global routing

00:09:43.627 --> 00:09:45.210
and the concept of
our global network,

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.520
we're essentially able
to deploy within one VPC

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:48.480
multiple regions.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.180
So that actually allows you
to connect with one router

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:53.640
that you deploy in one region.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:56.580
You can actually reach natively
other regions within GCP.

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.980
So that means that we're able
to advertise prefixes that

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:00.920
would exist, let's say,
in a different region

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:03.120
than the region where the
Cloud Router is deployed.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.820
And you can actually ride the
backbone of Google's network

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.950
to provide connectivity
between these regions.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:09.660
You do not need to
duplicate your effort

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:11.040
in building separate
Cloud Router

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.930
instances in different regions.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:15.932
This is done natively through
our global backbone network.

00:10:15.932 --> 00:10:17.640
It's a flag that's
enabling Cloud Router.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:20.057
When you actually deploy this,
it's called global routing.

00:10:20.057 --> 00:10:22.770
It enables you to seamlessly
transition our network

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:25.618
and allow access
to remote regions

00:10:25.618 --> 00:10:26.910
from the Cloud Router's region.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:33.800
DNS is another one of our very
popular solutions as well.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:36.590
Customers would like
to deploy on-premise.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.625
They have on premise
already a DNS server

00:10:38.625 --> 00:10:40.250
where they have post
names that they're

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.180
resolving for their
private resources on-prem.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:46.580
And then they also want to
deploy resources in the cloud,

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:49.160
so they would use
Cloud DNS in the cloud,

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:52.652
specify specific A
records for different VMs

00:10:52.652 --> 00:10:54.110
that they spin up
within the cloud.

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:56.110
And they would want their
on-premise environment

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:58.340
to also resolve host
names in the cloud.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:00.088
So they would do that
via DNS forwarding.

00:11:00.088 --> 00:11:02.130
We have both inbound and
outbound DNS forwarding,

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:03.630
which actually
allows you to resolve

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:06.650
host names from on-premise
workloads to the cloud,

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:09.017
and also from the cloud
to on-premise workloads.

00:11:09.017 --> 00:11:11.350
And we can allow that through
the means of interconnect.

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.450
So via either a dedicated
or partner interconnect

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:15.200
or through VPN,
we're able to extend

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.830
that connectivity via the
means of DNS forwarding.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:24.478
Another great offering is
through the shared VPC model.

00:11:24.478 --> 00:11:26.270
So within Google Cloud
we're able to deploy

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:29.570
a model via shared VPC, which
essentially structures a host

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:31.880
project, which is essentially
the anchor for all

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:33.950
your networking resources
within the cloud.

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:36.410
So service projects do not
need to worry about networking.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:37.935
They just need to
consume resources.

00:11:37.935 --> 00:11:39.560
They can be development
teams that just

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:40.727
want to deploy applications.

00:11:40.727 --> 00:11:43.018
They don't need to know about
the underlying networking

00:11:43.018 --> 00:11:46.130
infrastructure, the routing, the
subnets, the firewall policies.

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:48.470
This is all done by a
centralized networking project

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:50.428
that would be managed by
generally a networking

00:11:50.428 --> 00:11:52.040
team within your organization.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:55.490
And that project would deploy
Cloud Router within a region,

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:57.440
and then that Cloud
Router can essentially

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.120
share the resources
of the interconnect

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:00.662
with the individual
service projects.

00:12:00.662 --> 00:12:03.590
So the service project would
consume networking resources

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:07.160
similar to subnets that you
would grant them access to.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:09.890
And they can natively route
with any routing that's

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:11.780
available within
the host project

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:13.888
to your on-premise environment.

00:12:13.888 --> 00:12:15.680
So again, this really
simplifies the model.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.830
You don't need to duplicate
multiple interconnections

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:19.795
in each project.

00:12:19.795 --> 00:12:21.170
You can actually
centralize this,

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:22.712
have a central
network administration

00:12:22.712 --> 00:12:25.400
team that manages the
networking for your environment.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.860
And the rest of the teams that
need to manage development

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:30.050
projects or other types
of service projects

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:31.730
do not need to worry
about networking.

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:33.350
And they're essentially
connected back

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:36.110
to your on-premise environment
via policies that you set.

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:42.820
Another use case is connecting
privately to Google services.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:45.480
Google has many APIs
that can be exposed.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.990
For example, Cloud Storage
or BigQuery that you may

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:49.670
want to utilize from on-premise.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:52.400
And also because you have
a dedicated link to Google,

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:53.960
it's a good benefit
to be able to use

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:55.820
that link with low
latency access,

00:12:55.820 --> 00:12:59.000
high throughput to be able to
store data to let's say a Cloud

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:01.970
Storage bucket or to load
data onto BigQuery services.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:06.022
So you can actually
advertise by a VIP range

00:13:06.022 --> 00:13:07.730
back to your on-premise
environment, that

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:09.170
API that you can call.

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:12.470
And then you can actually make
calls to Google Cloud's API

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:15.360
services that are
available through this API.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:16.943
So this supports
Google API endpoints.

00:13:16.943 --> 00:13:18.443
There's a subset
of endpoints that's

00:13:18.443 --> 00:13:19.720
currently supported with this.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:21.595
And essentially on-premise,
what you would do

00:13:21.595 --> 00:13:24.920
is you do a DNS change
to rewrite the Google API

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:28.700
domain to use a specific
VIP, a VIP range that

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:31.400
allows you to connect
to these prefixes

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:32.720
through an interconnect link.

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:35.160
And you would
advertise that prefix,

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:37.470
which you can see in
this example here,

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:42.500
it's 199.36.153.4/30 on
the Cloud Router back

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:43.820
to your on-premise environment.

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:45.170
So the Cloud Router
actually allows you

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.420
to program which routes you
would want to advertise.

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:49.940
And by advertising this range,
making the necessary DNS

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:51.710
changes, you can
actually connect back

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:55.371
to on-prem to Google
API services natively.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:01.910
And let's talk a bit about
high availability topologies.

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:03.860
So one circuit, as
I mentioned earlier,

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:05.780
does not give you any SLAs.

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:09.000
To have an SLA, you need to have
two circuits for redundancy.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:11.480
So in this case, we have
a co-location facility,

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:13.730
which is located in
one metropolitan area.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:16.620
That metropolitan area would
have two availability zones.

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:19.970
So to have any SLA, a
three-nine SLA with us,

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:21.590
you would need to
connect across two

00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:23.120
of these metropolitan
availability

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.250
zones in a single metro.

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:27.890
So with BGP graceful
restart and Cloud Router,

00:14:27.890 --> 00:14:31.130
we're able to provide means
of having high availability

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:34.480
across these two zones.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:37.580
Now for customers that require
very high availability,

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:39.410
we would want to
duplicate that effort

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:41.178
across two metropolitan areas.

00:14:41.178 --> 00:14:43.220
So in this case, you may
have a metropolitan area

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.440
on the East Coast, maybe
one on the West Coast.

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:48.590
And you would be again connected
across two availability

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:50.870
zones within these
metropolitan areas.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:53.330
And that would provide you
a four-nine SLA with us

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:55.190
to connect back to GCP.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:57.560
And within GCP, you would
deploy a Cloud Router

00:14:57.560 --> 00:15:00.020
in one region and a Cloud
Router in the other region,

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:03.200
generally as close as possible
to where you are co-located

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:03.950
geographically.

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:04.970
So if it's on the
West Coast, you

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:06.380
would pick a region
on the West Coast.

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:08.297
And on the East Coast,
you would pick a region

00:15:08.297 --> 00:15:09.295
on the East Coast.

00:15:09.295 --> 00:15:10.670
With the means of
global routing,

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:12.350
as I mentioned
earlier, we're actually

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:15.260
able then to natively transit
our network in case of failure.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:17.420
So if we were to lose
an entire region,

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:19.580
we're able around
via the other region

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:21.830
to connect back to your
on-premise environment

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:23.980
via this model of deployment.

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:29.940
And now I'd like to introduce
my colleague Sasha on stage

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:34.100
to talk about high
availability Cloud VPN.

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:38.800
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:40.580
SASHA GONTMAKHER: Hi, I'm Sasha.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:44.560
I'm one of the engineers
at the VPN team.

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:47.170
And if there is one thing
I learned in my years

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:49.810
working for VPN, it
is that reliability

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:52.870
is a critical feature
of VPN because if you're

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:57.130
connectivity from your on-prem
location to GCP is down,

00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:00.140
you'll feel it across
all the organization.

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:03.790
Now the protocol that
we use to run VPN

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:06.430
is the industry
standard called IPsec.

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:10.330
It's pretty good when you
consider its encryption

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:13.360
capabilities, but
its ability to detect

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:17.000
and route around
failures is not so good.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:22.960
So if anything fails on the
way from GCP to your location,

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:25.400
then your tunnel will be toast.

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:32.720
And we want to build a
more reliable connection.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:37.090
And to do that, the way
is to use redundancy.

00:16:37.090 --> 00:16:39.580
So you can set up
several tunnels

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:42.310
running in parallel
between the two locations,

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:46.060
and hoping that if one is down
the other route should be up.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:50.830
So that gives you somebody,
but that's not enough.

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:54.580
Because if those tunnels
share any component

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.130
on the way from GCP
to your location,

00:16:57.130 --> 00:16:59.410
if there is a fault
in that component,

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:02.140
again, your whole
connection will be down.

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:06.130
So not only you need to
provide a redundant connection,

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:08.800
you need to be able
to isolate failure

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:12.400
by making sure that
at no stage there

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:16.720
is a single point of failure in
which your tunnels is working.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:20.200
Now us being cloud, this
is virtualized hardware,

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:22.150
so where do you even start?

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:26.230
So to do that, we
developed a new VPN product

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:28.780
called high availability VPN.

00:17:28.780 --> 00:17:34.510
And for that, we have a new
cloud VPN gateway, slightly

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:36.100
different from the old one.

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:41.140
And what it does, it provides
you with two interfaces.

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:44.500
And you can think of them
as two different network

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:49.250
cards, similar to
the physical network

00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:50.870
cards you would put
on your computer.

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:52.860
So if one of them is
burnt out, the others

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:55.580
are still continuing
to run your traffic.

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:56.860
But this is virtual.

00:17:56.860 --> 00:18:00.460
So if you have two
tunnels attached

00:18:00.460 --> 00:18:03.070
to two of the interfaces,
we will make sure

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:04.510
that they share nothing.

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:08.620
They share no line cards,
no network connections,

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:10.480
no machines, no switches.

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:14.350
Actually, being cloud, we are
pretty sure that they will not

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.090
even share a data center.

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:17.890
So they are pretty
much separated.

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:20.170
And if one of them
goes down, the other

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:25.100
is still assured and very
likely to be working.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:29.920
And then if a further scenario
happens, then a short time

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.800
after that, the
system will discover

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:36.190
that one tunnel went unhealthy
and switch all the traffic over

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:38.160
to the healthy tunnel.

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:43.370
Now how much exactly that gives
you in terms of reliability,

00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:44.440
it's a good question.

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:47.050
It's a function of how
good is the isolation

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:50.470
and how fast is the
process of switching over

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:53.410
from unhealthy tunnel
to the healthy one.

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:56.140
And we did this
analysis for our system.

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:57.550
And I'm happy to
announce that we

00:18:57.550 --> 00:19:00.370
are going to provide
four nines, which

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:05.300
is 99.99 regional availability.

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:09.670
So it means four and 1/2 minutes
of maximum monthly downtime,

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:13.870
or 53 minutes yearly.

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:16.130
And just a few notes.

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:18.040
So this is a
regional product, you

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:21.010
do not need to go
multi-regional to have

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:22.450
this level of reliability.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:24.640
Just one region is enough.

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:27.340
You will be able to connect
just as with classic VPN

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:28.520
that we had.

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:30.150
You'll be able to
connect both GCP

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.850
to GCP, or to your
on-prem locations,

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:35.530
or even to another cloud.

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:39.490
The only requirement
that is mandatory now

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:41.740
and was optional
with classic VPN

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:44.470
is that we need the
VPN on the other side

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:46.150
to support dynamic routing.

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:47.980
If you think of it,
it makes total sense

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:51.400
because you want your system
to be able to failover,

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:53.660
to switch the traffic
from one path to another.

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:55.510
So it's natural.

00:19:55.510 --> 00:20:00.490
And one last thing to
notice is that when

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:04.510
you're providing these
redundant connection,

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:08.140
you're actually having
twice as many tunnels

00:20:08.140 --> 00:20:12.470
as you're sure to have in case
there is any system failure.

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:16.840
So if you watch the
observable capacity

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:20.770
that you have in your bandwidth,
then it will be twice as much

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:23.920
as you will have assured.

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:28.990
And so if there is any failure,
then your available bandwidth

00:20:28.990 --> 00:20:33.130
will go down by a factor of
two, and that's not the time

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:36.190
when you want to discover
that you actually needed

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:39.280
more tunnels than
you have configured.

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:43.120
So to have a consistent
experience of your bandwidth

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:46.420
staying constant all the
time, what we recommend

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:49.870
is to set up a VPN connection
in an active passive

00:20:49.870 --> 00:20:53.590
configuration, meaning that one
of the tunnels has priority,

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:57.760
and all the traffic flows
through it unless that fails.

00:20:57.760 --> 00:21:01.750
And all the traffic switches
over to the other tunnel.

00:21:01.750 --> 00:21:04.320
With dynamic routing again,
that's easy to set up.

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:07.190
You just use different
MED values, and that's it.

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:11.350
So to recap, we're
going to provide

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:13.120
four-nines availability.

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:15.010
This system is
currently in alpha,

00:21:15.010 --> 00:21:17.860
it's going to launch
to beta next month.

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:21.730
And yeah, here's to more
traffic running more smoothly.

00:21:21.730 --> 00:21:23.740
Nick, back to you.

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:24.240
Thank you.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:25.587
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:25.587 --> 00:21:27.420
NICOLAS DE CRISTOFARO:
Thank you, thank you.

00:21:32.420 --> 00:21:34.460
So putting all that
into perspective now,

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:37.220
I will actually go
through a demo that

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:39.230
looks at a common
enterprise use case

00:21:39.230 --> 00:21:42.950
where we have an enterprise
that starts with a VPN tunnel.

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:45.380
Here, for example, I
have a customer project,

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:47.510
I have an IPsec
tunnel connected back

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:49.460
to a customer router on-prem.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:51.017
I set, as Sasha
was talking about,

00:21:51.017 --> 00:21:52.850
the MED values, which
are important in terms

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:54.590
of traffic engineering
that we're doing

00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:56.430
within the interconnect model.

00:21:56.430 --> 00:22:01.770
So in this case, I have a
standard MED value of 100.

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:03.940
And then I would like to
set up another connection.

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:06.260
This other connection would
be a dedicated or partner

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:07.593
interconnect offering.

00:22:07.593 --> 00:22:10.260
And then I don't want traffic to
switch over to this connection,

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:11.700
so I create a connection.

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:14.900
Create it with a lower
MED value of 1,000.

00:22:14.900 --> 00:22:16.340
And I establish a link.

00:22:16.340 --> 00:22:18.020
Now once that link
is established,

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:20.360
traffic will not
seamlessly switch over yet

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:22.070
because the value of
the MED priorities

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:24.590
are preferred over
the VPN tunnel

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:28.940
than from the
interconnect connection.

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:32.130
Now to do this traffic
switchover, it's very simple,

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:35.688
we just switch the MED values
between the VPN connection

00:22:35.688 --> 00:22:37.730
and the interconnect
connection by swapping them.

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:39.890
And then the traffic
seamlessly moves over.

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:42.980
So now I have a VPN connection
that can be used as a backup.

00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:44.955
I can keep it online,
in case I need it.

00:22:44.955 --> 00:22:46.580
But right now my
traffic would actually

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:50.660
be routed across a dedicated or
connected partner interconnect

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:51.160
link.

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:56.480
So here's the topology of what
we're doing for this demo.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:00.195
So I actually have a HA VPN
deployed with two interfaces.

00:23:00.195 --> 00:23:02.320
They're both deployed and
active/passive right now,

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:05.140
with MED value of
100, MED value of 200.

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:07.320
So active/passive
on the VPN link.

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:09.010
And then I have my
interconnect links

00:23:09.010 --> 00:23:12.377
with higher MED values
1,000 and 1,200.

00:23:12.377 --> 00:23:14.710
And they're connected back
to an on-premise environment,

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.680
which I'm running a ping
test between that and a VM

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:19.840
within GCP.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:23.110
So you can see here, this my
Cloud console with this project

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:24.990
that I just showed the topology.

00:23:24.990 --> 00:23:29.200
So we have here the interconnect
session and the VPN sessions.

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:31.900
You can see here the 1,000 and
the 1,200 as advertised route

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:32.930
priority.

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:37.030
And we can see here the 100
and 200 for my VPN connections,

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:38.170
my VPN tunnels.

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:48.180
So here I'm running a ping test
between an on-prem workload

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:49.650
and then a VM in GCP.

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:52.670
As you can see here,
we have sporadic pings

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:54.470
between 20 to 30 millisecond.

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:57.170
This is running across the
internet, this is a VPN tunnel.

00:23:57.170 --> 00:23:59.750
It's unpredictable due to the
path nature of the internet.

00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:02.043
So I can't really rely on
some guarantee [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:02.043 --> 00:24:02.585
milliseconds.

00:24:02.585 --> 00:24:05.570
It's pretty sporadic,
it's flipping up and down.

00:24:05.570 --> 00:24:07.190
I want to now
switch traffic over

00:24:07.190 --> 00:24:10.957
to a dedicated interconnect
link to have higher throughput

00:24:10.957 --> 00:24:11.915
and also lower latency.

00:24:14.123 --> 00:24:16.290
So here I'm just running a
script, which is actually

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:17.970
going to set the
priorities, swap them

00:24:17.970 --> 00:24:22.507
between the interconnect
and the VPN tunnels.

00:24:22.507 --> 00:24:24.840
So really what I'm doing is
I'm switching the priorities

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:29.100
both on the VPN side in GCP,
both VPN and interconnect

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.860
side on GCP and also on-prem
from my on-prem router

00:24:31.860 --> 00:24:33.960
to make sure that we're
updating on both sides

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:35.500
so that it's
symmetrical traffic.

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:38.910
Of course it's important to
do that so that traffic runs

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:42.540
both symmetrically
between the peers.

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:44.207
And once that's
actually completed,

00:24:44.207 --> 00:24:45.540
then we should see that traffic.

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:47.790
The latency is going to be
reduced quite significantly

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:51.483
because we're going to go across
a dedicated interconnect link

00:24:51.483 --> 00:24:53.400
with much lower latency
and higher throughput.

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:56.190
And as you can see now, we've
actually lowered it down.

00:24:56.190 --> 00:24:58.500
Once it stabilizes,
should be around eight,

00:24:58.500 --> 00:25:01.780
nine milliseconds when
traffic is running

00:25:01.780 --> 00:25:02.780
across the interconnect.

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:04.665
As you can see now,
it's nine milliseconds.

00:25:04.665 --> 00:25:07.530
So that traffic
seamlessly moved over

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:10.160
to the interconnect link
instead of the VPN tunnel.

00:25:15.610 --> 00:25:17.665
And as you can see,
I've seamlessly

00:25:17.665 --> 00:25:18.540
transitioned traffic.

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:19.800
There's no packet loss.

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:20.377
Easy to do.

00:25:20.377 --> 00:25:21.960
Now you can move
traffic over, and you

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:24.240
have traffic riding across
the interconnect link.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:34.810
And if we look at the Cloud
console and we refresh it,

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:37.030
now we see that the
priorities have been swapped,

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:39.160
so the interconnect session
now has 100 and 200.

00:25:39.160 --> 00:25:41.260
That's the preferred
path, active/passive path

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:42.520
across the interconnect.

00:25:42.520 --> 00:25:45.160
And 1,000 and 1,200
for the VPN tunnels.

00:25:45.160 --> 00:25:47.580
So they're still there, in
case we need it as a backup.

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:49.040
In case there
would be a failure,

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:52.160
we can still transition traffic
across the interconnect.

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:54.820
So it's that simple to be able
to do a migration between VPN

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:57.250
and interconnect seamlessly
between your enterprise

00:25:57.250 --> 00:25:59.170
and GCP.

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.940
And with that, that
is the end of my demo.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:04.960
I'd like to introduce Steve
Olson from the Home Depot,

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:07.080
and talk about his
interconnect experience.

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:07.880
Thank you.

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:11.800
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:17.393
STEVE OLSON: Hey,
my name is Steve,

00:26:17.393 --> 00:26:18.560
but everyone calls me Stevo.

00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:20.950
So you'll have to
excuse me, my voice

00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:22.840
is a little bit under
the weather today.

00:26:22.840 --> 00:26:25.970
Caught something here,
but we will push forward.

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:30.220
I've been with the Home
Depot for 12, 13 years, which

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:31.840
means I'm incredibly
tired and I've

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:33.630
seen lots of different things.

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:36.490
We like to say, we've
seen a lot of changes

00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:38.530
in Home Depot in
the last five years,

00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:42.120
probably more than we've
seen in the past 10 or 15.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:44.620
And that really goes to speak
to our digital transformation,

00:26:44.620 --> 00:26:45.970
things that we've done.

00:26:45.970 --> 00:26:48.122
We've really put IT first.

00:26:48.122 --> 00:26:50.080
It wasn't always the case
when I first started.

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:51.820
IT was a backseat.

00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:54.100
Now IT is a front seat
driver, and we've really

00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:56.720
done the strategy called
interconnected retail.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:58.660
That's really an
omni-channel strategy

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:02.470
that lets our customers buy
online, pick up in store,

00:27:02.470 --> 00:27:05.380
buy online, ship to their
home, buy in the store,

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:08.120
all that merges together to
give the customer the choice.

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:13.270
And one of the big strategies
that we played with first

00:27:13.270 --> 00:27:14.470
was the cloud.

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:18.010
GCP, we chose to
do e-com for it,

00:27:18.010 --> 00:27:19.450
and we have a
journey during that

00:27:19.450 --> 00:27:21.850
used the tools that the guys
have been talking about,

00:27:21.850 --> 00:27:23.830
and how we connect it to GCP.

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:26.530
But first, I want to start with
some basic facts about Home

00:27:26.530 --> 00:27:27.137
Depot.

00:27:27.137 --> 00:27:28.720
I won't read everything
on this slide,

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:31.600
but Home Depot was
founded in 1978.

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:36.196
Last year we did a
$108 billion in sales.

00:27:36.196 --> 00:27:38.740
Dot-com was up 22%
I think in sales.

00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:42.400
And there's some dot-com
guys here in front.

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:45.050
I think in the last
five years, six years,

00:27:45.050 --> 00:27:47.680
we've actually increased
dot-com sales by $1 billion

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:49.270
almost every single year.

00:27:49.270 --> 00:27:52.570
A lot of that growth has
been in our cloud space.

00:27:52.570 --> 00:27:54.070
We completely
re-platformed dot-com,

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:56.510
and we'll talk about
that in just a second.

00:27:56.510 --> 00:27:59.110
We have over 150 supply
chain facilities.

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:00.940
We have over 2,000
stores spread out

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:04.080
across Canada, US, and Mexico.

00:28:04.080 --> 00:28:06.723
And of course
400,000 associates.

00:28:06.723 --> 00:28:08.140
What a lot of
people don't realize

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:10.120
is we do have a big IT presence.

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:12.670
We have over 3,000
IT associates.

00:28:12.670 --> 00:28:15.845
We hired just under 1,000 last
year, and we're still going.

00:28:15.845 --> 00:28:17.470
We have offices all
across the country,

00:28:17.470 --> 00:28:20.510
from our various brands that
define Home Depot and companies

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:21.880
that we use.

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:25.000
And with that, I'll start
with our actual strategy

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:28.460
and how we started
with the cloud.

00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:30.910
So what led up to
a distributed model

00:28:30.910 --> 00:28:34.570
for us is really just starting
with e-commerce first.

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:39.350
And around 2014 we'd completely
re-platformed the website.

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:43.120
And it was decided that we
would go from an on-prem model

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:44.822
to in the cloud.

00:28:44.822 --> 00:28:46.780
And some of the reasons
are the classic reasons

00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:48.340
that you would hear.

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:50.350
We were building our
on-premise infrastructure

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:53.590
for sales that would be
around Thanksgiving or Black

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:55.540
Friday or Father's Day.

00:28:55.540 --> 00:28:57.670
And you build this
infrastructure to the point

00:28:57.670 --> 00:29:00.130
where you're using it
for those days only,

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:02.750
and then the rest of the
year it sits stagnant.

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:05.230
So we really wanted a more
on-demand infrastructure

00:29:05.230 --> 00:29:06.340
that could scale.

00:29:06.340 --> 00:29:08.830
And of course the
cloud came into that.

00:29:08.830 --> 00:29:10.328
It took multiple years to do.

00:29:10.328 --> 00:29:12.370
Really, this is going to
be a condensed timeline,

00:29:12.370 --> 00:29:14.500
but I'll explain exactly
how we got there.

00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:17.110
One of the first things
is data movement.

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:20.782
How do we actually get data from
our on-prem to the GCP Cloud.

00:29:20.782 --> 00:29:22.240
And the first thing,
of course, is,

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:24.198
before we even get going
with lots of projects,

00:29:24.198 --> 00:29:25.750
is just the basic internet.

00:29:25.750 --> 00:29:28.160
So we have basic
proxies and internet.

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:29.785
And that doesn't
scale incredibly well.

00:29:29.785 --> 00:29:32.118
What if you need private
services, just like they talked

00:29:32.118 --> 00:29:33.480
about in the slides before me--

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:36.220
VPNs and interconnects.

00:29:36.220 --> 00:29:40.000
We first started
using VPNs, and I've

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:42.550
got a love/hate
relationship with VPNs.

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:45.100
Some people like
them, I hate VPNs,

00:29:45.100 --> 00:29:47.420
and I'll explain that
for a few reasons.

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:49.240
I mean, VPNs suck,
let's face it.

00:29:49.240 --> 00:29:52.390
But if that's all you can do,
that's all you can do, right?

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:54.100
We actually use static routes.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:55.750
There is no Cloud
Router at this point.

00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:58.900
This is around 2015, so we
were stuck with static routes

00:29:58.900 --> 00:30:00.120
and no redundancy.

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:03.850
And it worked, except it
terminated on-prem here,

00:30:03.850 --> 00:30:06.450
and then it terminated
inside the cloud.

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:09.052
It basically did
what it needed to do.

00:30:09.052 --> 00:30:10.510
But we needed
something more robust

00:30:10.510 --> 00:30:12.600
and we needed to scale high.

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:15.500
And so the problem was
with VPN, and which

00:30:15.500 --> 00:30:16.810
I said we'll talk about MTU.

00:30:16.810 --> 00:30:19.630
How many of you have ever dealt
with frame size issues and MTU

00:30:19.630 --> 00:30:21.220
problems with VPN.

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:22.700
Everyone's hand is
going up, right?

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:24.280
MTU is a terrible
thing to deal with,

00:30:24.280 --> 00:30:25.600
and it's not Google's fault.

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:28.360
But Google, you have
to have fragmentation

00:30:28.360 --> 00:30:29.350
before encapsulation.

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:33.190
And if you've ever dealt with
firewalls and packet captures

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:35.470
and wire captures for why
something doesn't work--

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:37.570
does your application even
support fragmentation.

00:30:37.570 --> 00:30:39.948
Doesn't it know it's
going to be fragmented?

00:30:39.948 --> 00:30:41.740
I just need an Advil
just talking about it.

00:30:41.740 --> 00:30:44.920
We dealt with that so long that
we needed to get rid of that.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:47.213
Speed and latency is
another big thing.

00:30:47.213 --> 00:30:49.630
One of the big things that I
found about speed and latency

00:30:49.630 --> 00:30:51.485
is that if you talk
to your customers,

00:30:51.485 --> 00:30:53.860
sometimes they don't know the
difference between that is.

00:30:53.860 --> 00:30:57.280
They might say, I need something
quick, but does quick mean,

00:30:57.280 --> 00:30:59.560
I need to load a terabyte
of data in a day,

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:02.440
or I need a connection to a
database in five milliseconds.

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:04.898
Sometimes you have to actually
lead that conversation there

00:31:04.898 --> 00:31:07.030
to see exactly what
they care about.

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:10.677
SLA and the internet in general,
that's a big thing as well.

00:31:10.677 --> 00:31:13.135
You come in one day and your
connection across the internet

00:31:13.135 --> 00:31:15.267
is 30 milliseconds, and
the next day it's 45.

00:31:15.267 --> 00:31:16.350
We've all dealt with that.

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:19.980
We actually made a diagram that
basically show cold potato,

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:22.790
hot potato routing to explain
to our application developers

00:31:22.790 --> 00:31:25.400
and our consumers what
it's like that, yes, we

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:27.830
have internet
connections to the cloud,

00:31:27.830 --> 00:31:29.630
but we don't go straight there.

00:31:29.630 --> 00:31:31.673
I don't own the
infrastructure in the middle.

00:31:31.673 --> 00:31:33.590
And getting their head
wrapped around the fact

00:31:33.590 --> 00:31:35.690
that, yes, I may
leave here, but I

00:31:35.690 --> 00:31:37.880
don't know what some
tier 1 provider does

00:31:37.880 --> 00:31:39.300
with it after the fact.

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:42.960
And that's how we
explain that to them.

00:31:42.960 --> 00:31:45.170
So let's see deal with
the VPN issue first.

00:31:45.170 --> 00:31:49.070
So we really wanted to have a
way to have the VPN be better.

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:50.790
We couldn't fix the
MTU immediately,

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:53.280
but we can fix some of the
speed and latency problems.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:54.697
So this is where
we really started

00:31:54.697 --> 00:31:56.430
discussing peering facilities.

00:31:56.430 --> 00:31:58.850
So we actually
came up with, if we

00:31:58.850 --> 00:32:01.340
could get the VPNs
closer to Google,

00:32:01.340 --> 00:32:03.020
we can actually get
performance better.

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:06.840
So speed, latency,
reliability, SLA.

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:09.740
And so we came up with
peering centers on the East

00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:12.440
and on the West Coast.

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:14.680
Those are the first good
choices because a lot

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:17.180
of the infrastructure for things
that we're using are there.

00:32:17.180 --> 00:32:18.805
And we have well-defined
latencies now.

00:32:18.805 --> 00:32:20.610
So instead of
using the internet,

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:22.990
we own these private circuits
between some of our data

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:25.130
centers directly to
these peering centers.

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:28.250
So I can tell my application
developers and all my teams,

00:32:28.250 --> 00:32:30.920
I know what you're going to
get from a latency perspective.

00:32:30.920 --> 00:32:33.950
I know this is 15,
I know this is 40.

00:32:33.950 --> 00:32:36.480
And we don't have to
worry about the internet.

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:39.410
So what we really did,
revisiting the first slide,

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:43.390
is we moved the VPN connections
all the way out to the co-lo

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:45.543
there in the peering
centers, and the VPN

00:32:45.543 --> 00:32:46.710
is still inside the project.

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:52.250
So the internet is not used, but
reliability and SLA go way up.

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:54.300
Speed is now
actually even better,

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:56.200
because now we have
direct connections.

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:58.485
So we're running the VPN
basically on the last mile.

00:32:58.485 --> 00:33:00.110
We're not running it
across the country

00:33:00.110 --> 00:33:01.370
and hoping that it gets there.

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:04.100
We have our own private
circuits that gets us there.

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:07.210
So the real redundancy
actually still isn't here yet.

00:33:07.210 --> 00:33:10.910
So we actually started
doing multiple VPNs,

00:33:10.910 --> 00:33:12.890
but without Cloud
Router and static routes

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:15.100
and trying to scale that.

00:33:15.100 --> 00:33:16.100
That didn't work either.

00:33:16.100 --> 00:33:19.220
So we needed to do something
even better than that.

00:33:19.220 --> 00:33:21.440
It actually worked well
enough that we basically

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:22.760
started adding more.

00:33:22.760 --> 00:33:25.200
People liked it so much
that we added a couple more.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:27.240
So now we have seven
milliseconds and three.

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:30.080
We actually have vendors
and application owners

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:32.330
that test this and can get
connections straight to GCP

00:33:32.330 --> 00:33:34.250
within 10 milliseconds.

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:38.720
So once we started doing that,
then interconnect beta started.

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:40.490
And if we can get
the interconnect on,

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:42.600
we can get rid of the VPNs.

00:33:42.600 --> 00:33:46.100
So now we're going
to fix the VPN issue.

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:47.730
That's still a
problem with the MTUs.

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:50.360
So what happens is the
interconnect beta comes,

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:53.180
Cloud Router comes, and we
have now an interconnect

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:54.608
plus a direct connect.

00:33:54.608 --> 00:33:56.150
So at each of the
peering facilities,

00:33:56.150 --> 00:33:57.400
we have four different cables.

00:33:57.400 --> 00:34:00.680
We have two for public
access, two for interconnect,

00:34:00.680 --> 00:34:02.540
and depending on what
the project needs are,

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:04.040
they can do private
access or they

00:34:04.040 --> 00:34:06.410
can do public access over
our own infrastructure

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:06.980
to get there.

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:08.880
The internet is not involved.

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:10.580
And of course with
dynamic routing,

00:34:10.580 --> 00:34:12.290
the redundancy is
much more seamless

00:34:12.290 --> 00:34:15.300
and it fails over much easier.

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:18.230
So with that, we
basically grew even more.

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:20.920
We actually added another
facility this year.

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:22.730
And all these sites
back each other up.

00:34:22.730 --> 00:34:25.580
They're talking about
mission critical reliability.

00:34:25.580 --> 00:34:28.190
We built this in such a way
that when we have circuit issues

00:34:28.190 --> 00:34:30.949
and some of these go down, our
application owners don't even

00:34:30.949 --> 00:34:31.969
notice.

00:34:31.969 --> 00:34:34.040
Because if they're
multi-region and multi-tasked

00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:36.949
to different interconnects
and use Cloud Routers,

00:34:36.949 --> 00:34:38.960
it automatically fails over.

00:34:38.960 --> 00:34:44.120
So we've scaled to there now,
but we're even adding more.

00:34:44.120 --> 00:34:46.340
And really it's not
just GCP either.

00:34:46.340 --> 00:34:49.070
The journey to actually getting
us connected directly to GCP

00:34:49.070 --> 00:34:50.389
led us to other things.

00:34:50.389 --> 00:34:52.228
We're using it for
other providers as well

00:34:52.228 --> 00:34:53.520
and other vendors and partners.

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:55.280
It wasn't even the
internet itself.

00:34:55.280 --> 00:34:57.570
So just the concept
of getting to GCP

00:34:57.570 --> 00:35:00.200
led us to this distributed
regionalized network

00:35:00.200 --> 00:35:01.520
that we can use.

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:05.870
And with the combination of
new technologies like SD-WAN

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:07.850
and things like
that, we can have

00:35:07.850 --> 00:35:10.910
our remote sites, our stores,
our distribution centers.

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:13.450
We can have them all come to
the closest peering facility

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:16.190
and use cloud services or
use the internet and things

00:35:16.190 --> 00:35:17.178
that they might need.

00:35:17.178 --> 00:35:19.220
They don't need to come
back to the main offices.

00:35:19.220 --> 00:35:20.720
They don't need to go
directly to the internet.

00:35:20.720 --> 00:35:22.928
We can control that all in
a centralized regionalized

00:35:22.928 --> 00:35:25.410
manner.

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:28.560
And so with that being
said, what we're doing next

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:30.870
is we're investigating what
we actually talked about

00:35:30.870 --> 00:35:33.510
in the last few slides,
it's private services

00:35:33.510 --> 00:35:35.130
via these channels.

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:38.310
Meaning using our private space
to contact BigQuery and things

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:39.220
like that.

00:35:39.220 --> 00:35:41.460
We're really interested
in shared VPC,

00:35:41.460 --> 00:35:43.920
and we're going to continue
deploying more peering centers

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:46.420
and keep using the
multi-region model and Cloud

00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:48.060
Routers and interconnects.

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:50.310
And we'll probably even
expand to more peering centers

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:52.080
in Canada and Mexico this year.

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:53.700
This model has proved
extremely well.

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:55.050
Our customers like it.

00:35:55.050 --> 00:35:56.620
And we will continue to do that.

00:35:56.620 --> 00:35:59.020
With that being said,
that is my talk.

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:03.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

